  "text": "**Solution 3 (q = 2)** is the most plausible of the three, but I could not verify it, and its argument has a gap.\n\n- **Solution 1** is internally inconsistent: its boxed value is q ≈ 2.6067, yet its \"Final answer\" line says 1. The Erdős–Borwein constant is also an unmotivated choice here, and its \"back-pressure\" recursions are asserted rather than derived.\n- **Solution 2** gets 1 + ln 2 ≈ 1.6931 from an integral over \"penetration exponents\" that is set up by assertion. The comb exponent 4/3 it derives is a lower bound on the explosion threshold, not the threshold itself, and the optimization over architectures is not justified.\n- **Solution 3** gives q = 2. Its upper bound relies on V(d) ≥ d and on a flux-per-site heuristic that measures ball volume around o. On a comb, mass at distance d must fill the region along the spine path, not a ball around o, so the argument does not cover all bounded-degree graphs. The Z computation (divergence of Σ d^{1−p} exactly when p ≤ 2) is at least self-consistent. I also have a low-confidence recollection that a second-moment threshold appears in the literature on this model, but I could not confirm it.\n\nSo I'm choosing Solution 3 with low confidence, and none of the three is established by its own reasoning.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
